  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Data view exploration:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` has `timestamp` as its time field. The key fields are `response.keyword` (status codes), `clientip`, `bytes`, `geo.dest` (country), `url`, `extension`, and `agent`. There are only three status codes: 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801), and 503 (441). The only 5xx code is 503.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-f87dae4c Web logs overview\" (`eff-f87dae4c-web-logs-overview-d072f6842135`) has six panels:\n- Total requests (metric)\n- Unique client IPs (metric)\n- Average response bytes (metric)\n- Requests over time (line, date histogram)\n- Responses by status code (pie)\n- Top 10 countries by requests (bar)\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-f87dae4c 5xx spike\" (`5240784e-010e-43ef-b8d1-90230b611414`) is an `.es-query` rule. It runs every 1m and counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5*` over a 5-minute window. It fires when the count is above 10. It is enabled and has no notification action attached, since none was requested.\n\nThings to know:\n- **Time range:** The sample timestamps run from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, which is after today (2026-10-11). I set the dashboard's time range to 2026-10-01 through 2026-12-31 so the panels show data. A default \"last 7 days\" view would be empty.\n- **Rule status:** The rule shows `pending`, not yet evaluated. Because the data is dated in the future, the 5-minute window may not match anything right now, so the rule may not fire soon.\n- **Verification:** I confirmed the dashboard's six panels with `get_dashboard` and the rule's enabled state with `get_alert_rule`. I did not render the dashboard in a browser.\n- **Elasticsearch mapping lookup:** The `get_mappings` call failed with \"error decoding response body\", so I relied on the Kibana data view fields instead.",
